\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Different domains for the form, elimination and trace ratio}
\date{2026-10-18}

\p{On the [arithmetic family](fdip-000U), a nondegenerate
full form need not permit a chosen elimination, and an
invertible effective form need not give a regular trace ratio.
With #{s=t+2}, #{f=t+1} and
#{\kappa=3(2t+1)(2t+3)}, the exact regular loci in #{X_q} are
##{\begin{aligned}
\text{effective form:}&\quad X_{\mathrm{elim}}=D_{X_q}(s),\\
\text{trace ratio:}&\quad U_R=D_{X_q}(f).
\end{aligned}}
The effective form is the quadratic form on the retained rank-2
summand obtained by eliminating the complementary summand. It is
regular exactly on #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}, and the trace ratio is
regular exactly on #{U_R}. Their common domain is
#{X_{\mathrm{obs}}=D_{X_q}(sf)}.
These claims follow from the calculations below, extending the
[real Schur example](fdip-000F) and
[trace-ratio calculation](fdip-000I) over the declared rings.
The [intrinsic trace ratio](fdip-003H) requires a unit trace:
#{\trace L=4f} is a unit exactly when #{f} is, since
#{2} is already inverted.}

\proof{
\p{Order coordinates by retained #{(0,2)} and eliminated
#{(1,3)}. The diagonal blocks of #{Q} are
#{A=C_{\mathrm{block}}=sI_2}, and the off-diagonal block is
##{B=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-t\\-t&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
On #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}, the Schur matrix is
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=A-BC_{\mathrm{block}}^{-1}B^T\\
&=\frac1s\begin{pmatrix}4t+3&-2t\\-2t&4t+3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{BB^T} has diagonal #{1+t^2} and off-diagonal #{2t},
and #{s^2-(1+t^2)=4t+3}. Moreover
##{\det S=\frac{(4t+3)^2-4t^2}{s^2}
=\frac{\kappa}{s^2},}
a unit in #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}. On #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}^2},
define #{q_{\mathrm{eff}}(y)=y^TSy}; its half-polar matrix is
#{S}. Thus both #{q} and #{q_{\mathrm{eff}}} are
nondegenerate on this open. On the positive real locus,
#{q_{\mathrm{eff}}/2} is the quadratic part of the effective
action; the Gaussian normalization constant is additional.}

\p{The domain #{R_q} embeds in its fraction field, so these
entries can be tested in each local ring. If a prime
#{\mathfrak p} contains #{s}, the off-diagonal numerator
#{-2t} is congruent to #{4} modulo #{\mathfrak p} and is
a unit in #{(R_q)_{\mathfrak p}}. If #{(-2t)/s} were
regular there, multiplication by #{s} would equate an element
of the maximal ideal with a unit. This is impossible.
On #{D_{X_q}(s)} every entry is regular. Hence this is the
exact regular locus of the displayed Schur matrix.}

\p{For #{L=Q-I}, the diagonalization in
\ref{fdip-000U} gives eigenvalues #{0,2,2t,2t+2}. Therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
\trace L&=4(t+1),\\
\trace(L^2)&=8(t^2+t+1),\\
R(t)&=\frac{\trace(L^2)}{(\trace L)^2}
=\frac{t^2+t+1}{2(t+1)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
The numerator is #{f^2-f+1}. At every prime containing #{f},
it has residue #{1}, so is a unit in the local ring.
Regularity of #{R} there would make
##{2f^2R=f^2-f+1}
an equality of an element of the maximal ideal and a unit,
again impossible. Where #{f} is invertible the formula is
regular. Thus the exact ratio locus is #{U_R}, and its
intersection with #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}} is
#{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}. The interpretation of these localizations
as regular functions is
\citet{Lemma 26.5.4}{stacks2017stacks}, [Tag 01HR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01HR).}
}

\p{These loci do not select a unique arithmetic realization.
Algebraic invertibility gives neither positivity nor a probability
measure over the arithmetic base, and an L-value comparison needs
additional structure. The block half-turn and the three rational
points are \ref{fdip-00LE}. The real positivity comparison and the
residue loss are \ref{fdip-00LF}.}
